# Source attribution and reuse

This is a source-attributed snapshot and structured adaptation of eDiAna:
Miller, Jared; Rieken, Elisabeth; Hackstein, Olav; Yakubovich, Ilya (eds.),
2017–. Digital Philological-Etymological Dictionary of the Minor Ancient
Anatolian Corpus Languages. https://www.ediana.gwi.uni-muenchen.de

Upstream data and project adaptations are CC BY-SA 4.0:
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/
Licence and citation evidence: https://www.ediana.gwi.uni-muenchen.de/assets/pdfs/citation.pdf
The full PDF checksum and exact licence statement are retained in
data/source-evidence/license.json. Provide the eDiAna hyperlink when reusing
these materials; the source's citation guidelines explicitly require it.

Catalogue and corpus responses were captured across 2026-10-01/02 UTC.
The selected catalogue labels are Lycian (2017, archival), Carian (2022),
and Lycian B / Milyan (2021). Retrieval times for each request are recorded
in data/acquisition.json. Source IDs are namespaced by language; the same
integer in different corpora does not identify the same entry.

The eDiAna team digitized and annotated these corpora. Its Lycian edition
adapts H. Craig Melchert's 2001 Lycian Corpus, based primarily on Kalinka
1901, Friedrich 1932 and Neumann 1979, with later inscriptions and readings
including contributions by Birgit Christiansen. Its Carian edition follows
Ignacio J. Adiego, The Carian Language (2007), with later additions. Individual
principal-edition references are preserved per entry. See the original
acknowledgments: https://www.ediana.gwi.uni-muenchen.de/corpus.php

Changes made here: archived public catalogue and responses; parsed line
strings and source annotation tables; preserved IDs, Unicode and line labels;
added conservative analytical exclusions, exact offsets, JSON/JSONL/CSV
exports, provenance, tests and audits. Source readings and source annotation
claims have not been independently verified. Upstream lemmata, translations,
parts of speech and morphology remain attributed upstream assertions.

Source response HTML tables remain in their original JSON snapshots.
Structured annotations retain duplicated columns used for clitic analyses;
columns are not additional independent word occurrences. Native signs and
Greek letters used in transliteration are not converted into proposed phonetic
values. Greek-script lines are separated conservatively by a documented
project screen, not a new authoritative linguistic reassignment.

Pinned family reports retain their original project attribution, rights and
gates. Links to external editions do not grant a new licence for those editions.
Original toolkit code: MIT, copyright 2026 hawkinsnick corpus contributors.
